{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Evidence-Based Reflection","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting evidence-based reflection to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"06","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/05"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Evidence-Based Reflection\n\n![Evidence-Based Reflection textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-06-evidence-based-reflection.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost teaching reflection is a record of feelings: the lesson \"went well,\" students \"seemed engaged,\" the tool \"really helped.\" Feelings are real, but they are not evidence, and a reflection built on them cannot tell you whether to keep doing the thing or stop. Evidence-based reflection replaces impression with a small, honest argument: here is what I claim happened, here is the data that supports it, here is the data that complicates it, here are the rival explanations I cannot rule out, and here is the check I will run next. It is the discipline that turns teaching from a series of vibes into a practice that learns.\n\nThis matters most for AI tools, where the experience of speed and novelty is especially seductive. A class can feel transformed by an AI feedback tool while the actual learning gain belongs to an easier quiz or a review day that happened the same week. The reflection that survives scrutiny is the one that went looking for the evidence against its own first impression. The Design Tension Studio map is the decision; the reflection is where you find out, with evidence, whether the decision was right.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher writes: \"The AI feedback unit went really well and students were engaged.\" A mentor asks two questions that the reflection cannot answer. What did you observe that a skeptic would accept as evidence? And what did you notice that argued against your conclusion? The teacher has reported a conclusion without the case for it, and (more revealingly) without any sign that she looked for disconfirming evidence. A reflection that only confirms its author's hopes is indistinguishable from wishful thinking.\n\nThe repair turns the impression into an argument. Exit-ticket scores rose from 58% to 81%, which is evidence. But the four students on the printed fallback scored twelve points below peers, which is counter-evidence the honest reflection must report. And the same week had a review day and an easier quiz, which are rival explanations she cannot yet dismiss. The reflection becomes credible precisely when it stops being flattering.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvidence-based reflection is the construction of a defeasible claim about your own teaching, built from four moves:\n\n- **Claim with data.** State what you think happened and attach the specific evidence: scores, work samples, observation counts, not \"engagement\" but the behaviors you would count as engagement.\n- **Seek disconfirming evidence.** Actively report the data that complicates the claim. The subgroup that did not gain, the student whose work got worse, the metric that stayed flat. A reflection that contains no counter-evidence has not yet been honest, because real classrooms are never uniformly successful.\n- **Weigh rival explanations.** Before crediting a gain to your tool, ask what else could have caused it. A review day, an easier assessment, regression to the mean, or your own increased attention to the topic are all rivals that must be considered before the tool gets the credit.\n- **Commit to a check.** End not with a verdict but with the next test that would distinguish your explanation from the rivals (for example, holding quiz difficulty constant next time).\n\nThis structure mirrors how evidence works in any inquiry: a claim is only as strong as the disconfirming evidence it survived and the alternatives it ruled out. The course anchors keep the reflection grounded. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) keeps the claim tied to a real learning target rather than to engagement theater; critical edtech studies (Selwyn, 2022) pushes the disconfirming move toward subgroup effects the average hides, a demand algorithmic-fairness research makes explicit: outcomes must be disaggregated by socially meaningful subgroups because an aggregate metric can mask disparate effects (Hanna et al., 2020); datafication reminds you that the tool's own analytics are a vendor-shaped data source, not neutral truth (Williamson, Macgilchrist, & Potter, 2023).\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reflection reads: 'The AI feedback unit went really well and students were engaged.' Which of the four moves is most clearly absent?\" explanation=\"The claim is attached to a feeling, not to specific data a skeptic would accept. 'Engaged' names an impression, not the counted behaviors that would serve as evidence. Without 'claim with data,' the reflection is wishful thinking, and it has also not sought disconfirming evidence.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It weighs too many rival explanations\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It attaches the claim to a feeling rather than to specific data\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It commits to a check that is too demanding\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It reports too much disconfirming evidence\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nWork through how a teacher turns an impression into a defeasible claim. Predict the next move before each reveal:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"From impression to evidence-based reflection\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Starting reflection: The lesson went really well and students were engaged. What is the first move that would make this inspectable?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Add a more confident concluding sentence\",\"Attach specific data: exit-ticket scores rose from 58% to 81%\",\"Switch to a different tool next week\",\"Ask students if they liked it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Claim with data: ’Exit-ticket scores rose from 58% to 81% across the class.’ Now the claim is attached to something a skeptic would accept rather than to a feeling.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You have the gain. What move does honest reflection require next, before celebrating?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Report disconfirming evidence, such as a subgroup that did not gain\",\"Round the gain up to 25 points\",\"Credit the tool immediately\",\"Stop, the data is enough\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Seek disconfirming evidence: ’The 4 students on the printed fallback averaged 12 points lower than peers, so the gain may not be shared evenly.’ A reflection with no counter-evidence has not yet been honest.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Before crediting the gain to the AI tool, what must you consider?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Whether students liked the interface\",\"Rival explanations: the same week had a review day and an easier quiz\",\"The vendor dashboard mastery figure\",\"Nothing else could explain it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Weigh rival explanations: a review day and an easier quiz are live alternative causes you cannot yet dismiss, so the tool does not yet get the credit.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Commit to a check: ’Next week I will hold quiz difficulty constant and compare the fallback group again before deciding whether to keep the tool.’ The reflection now cites data, names counter-evidence, weighs rivals, and ends with a test, which is what makes it inspectable rather than flattering.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with a thin claim that many students write on a first pass:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe stronger version treats the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision as evidence. It names what changed, links the change to **critical edtech studies**, and leaves a reader able to audit the decision.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Evidence-Based Reflection\n\nFollow a teacher revising a reflection on a unit taught with an AI feedback tool.\n\n1. **Before (impression).** \"The lesson went really well and students were engaged.\" A reviewer annotates: \"What did you observe, and what argued against your view?\"\n2. **Bring the data.** Revision: \"Exit-ticket scores rose from 58% to 81% across the class.\"\n3. **Report disconfirming evidence.** Add: \"The 4 students on the printed fallback averaged 12 points lower than peers on the tool, so the gain may not be shared evenly.\"\n4. **Check rival explanations.** The same week included a review day and an easier quiz, so the teacher notes she cannot yet credit the gain to the tool alone.\n5. **Stated next step (after).** \"Next week I will hold the quiz difficulty constant and compare the fallback group again before deciding whether to keep the tool.\"\n\nThe before reports a feeling; the after cites data, names counter-evidence, weighs rival causes, and commits to a check, which is what makes a reflection inspectable.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Does the vocabulary app deserve the credit?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Delayed-recall quiz scores rose from a class average of 62% to 74% the unit you piloted an AI vocabulary app. A colleague says: clearly the app works, roll it out everywhere. The same week you also started a daily five-minute review routine. What is your next move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Agree and recommend the app district-wide\",\"next\":\"premature\",\"feedback\":\"Crediting the gain to the tool before ruling out the review day is a causal claim the evidence does not yet license.\"},{\"label\":\"Disaggregate the gain by subgroup before drawing any conclusion\",\"next\":\"disaggregate\",\"feedback\":\"You went looking for the disconfirming evidence the average hides, which is the honest move.\"},{\"label\":\"Trust the app dashboard, which reports 90% mastery\",\"next\":\"vendor\",\"feedback\":\"The dashboard is a vendor-shaped data source, not neutral truth; its mastery figure may count in-app practice, not independent recall.\"}]},\"premature\":{\"prompt\":\"A skeptic asks: how do you know it was the app and not the new review routine? You cannot answer. What did you skip?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"I skipped weighing the rival explanation before crediting the tool\",\"next\":\"disaggregate\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Rule out the alternatives before attribution.\"}]},\"vendor\":{\"prompt\":\"You check what the 90% mastery actually measures: in-app practice attempts, not independent delayed recall. How should you treat this figure?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"As a claim to verify against my own assessment, not as evidence\",\"next\":\"disaggregate\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The vendor metric is a claim, not neutral truth.\"}]},\"disaggregate\":{\"prompt\":\"Disaggregating, you find the gain is concentrated in students who already scored high, while the lowest quartile barely moved. What does this counter-evidence imply for the tension-map equity decision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The app may be widening rather than closing the gap the map cared about\",\"next\":\"rival\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly: the class mean rose while the students the tool should help fell behind. The average hid the equity story.\"},{\"label\":\"The average rose, so the equity goal is met\",\"next\":\"meanhides\",\"feedback\":\"The mean hides the subgroup. A rising average can coexist with a widening gap.\"}]},\"meanhides\":{\"prompt\":\"Look past the mean to the lowest quartile specifically. What do you conclude?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The gap may be widening; report this counter-evidence honestly\",\"next\":\"rival\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Disconfirmation is a required move, not an optional virtue.\"}]},\"rival\":{\"prompt\":\"You now hold a gain, a subgroup counter-finding, and a live rival cause (the review routine). How should the reflection end?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"With a confident verdict: keep the app\",\"next\":\"premature\",\"feedback\":\"The evidence does not license a confident verdict yet; a review day still confounds it.\"},{\"label\":\"With a check: next unit run the review routine without the app in one section, and track the lowest quartile\",\"next\":\"check\",\"feedback\":\"A modest, evidence-anchored close that would actually distinguish your explanation from the rival.\"}]},\"check\":{\"prompt\":\"Your reflection ends in I do not yet know, and here is how I will find out. Why is that a stronger professional position than a confident endorsement?\",\"feedback\":\"A claim is only as strong as the disconfirming evidence it survived and the alternatives it ruled out. By citing the gain, reporting the subgroup that did not move, naming the review-routine rival, treating the vendor metric as a claim, and committing to a controlled check, the reflection becomes one a reader who wanted the opposite conclusion would still find fair. That fairness, not confidence, is what makes a reflection trustworthy.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nLook for the evidence against your conclusion before you write the conclusion. A reflection earns trust in proportion to the disconfirming evidence it reports and the rival explanations it rules out, not the confidence it projects. If your reflection contains no counter-evidence, assume you have not looked hard enough yet.\n\nRun any reflection through three questions:\n\n1. **Evidence check:** Is the claim attached to specific data a skeptic would accept, not to \"engagement\" or \"felt good\"?\n2. **Disconfirmation check:** Does it report at least one finding that complicates the claim, such as a subgroup that did not gain?\n3. **Rival check:** Have I considered what besides my tool could explain the result, and named the next test that would distinguish them?\n\nIf any answer is missing, the reflection is an impression dressed as a finding.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A teacher reports a class-average gain after an AI tool and concludes it worked. Explain how 'the average that hides the subgroup' can let a tool that worsened equity look like a success, and name the move that exposes it.\" answer=\"A class mean is a single number that can rise even while the students the tool was meant to help fall further behind, because high-scoring students can pull the average up enough to mask a declining lowest quartile. So a tool that widened the gap the tension map cared about can read as a uniform success, hiding the equity story entirely. The move that exposes it is disaggregation: report the subgroup data, especially the lowest quartile, as required disconfirming evidence rather than reporting only the mean. Critical edtech studies (Selwyn, 2022) pushes exactly this, turning the average into a question about who gained and who did not.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"learner_reflection\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe dominant error is confirmation: reporting only the evidence that supports the hoped-for conclusion and quietly ignoring the rest. This is rarely dishonesty; it is the natural pull of wanting a decision to have been right. The corrective is to make disconfirmation a required move rather than an optional virtue. A related error is the average that hides the subgroup: a class mean can rise while the students the tool was supposed to help fall further behind, and a reflection that reports only the mean misses the equity story entirely.\n\nThe subtlest misreading is the premature verdict. Crediting a gain to the tool before ruling out the review day and the easier quiz is a causal claim the evidence does not yet license. Honest reflection often ends in \"I do not yet know, and here is how I will find out,\" which is a stronger professional position than a confident attribution the data cannot support.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nA teacher reflects on whether an AI vocabulary app improved retention. The impression: \"Students loved it and seemed to remember more.\" The evidence-based version starts with data: delayed-recall quiz scores rose from a class average of 62% to 74%. Then disconfirmation: she disaggregates and finds the gain concentrated in students who already scored high, while the lowest quartile barely moved, so the app may be widening rather than closing the gap the tension map cared about.\n\nThen rivals: the app launched the same week she started a daily five-minute review routine, so the routine is a live alternative cause. And datafication: the app's own dashboard claims \"90% mastery,\" but that figure counts in-app practice, not independent recall, so she treats the vendor metric as a claim to verify rather than evidence. Her reflection concludes not with \"keep the app\" but with a check: next unit she will run the review routine without the app for one section to isolate the effect, and she will track the lowest quartile specifically. That conclusion is modest, evidence-anchored, and actually informative, which is everything an impression-based reflection is not.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a reflection, separate every sentence into impression (a feeling or unsupported conclusion) and evidence (a checkable observation). Then ask the two questions the author should have asked: what here argues against the conclusion, and what besides the intervention could explain the result. The missing answers reveal where the reflection stopped being honest. By the end of CAT 531 06.06 you should be able to read any teaching reflection and immediately tell whether its author went looking for the evidence against their own view.\n\nA strong reader of an evidence-based reflection can answer four questions:\n\n1. What specific data supports the claim, and would a skeptic accept it?\n2. What disconfirming evidence, especially at the subgroup level, did the author report?\n3. What rival explanations were considered before crediting the intervention?\n4. What concrete check would settle the open question next time?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA reflection is strong when a reader who wanted the opposite conclusion would still find the argument fair, because the disconfirming evidence and the rival explanations are on the page, not omitted. Confident prose is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are the data, the counter-evidence, and the named next check. If a claim has no disconfirming evidence beside it, mark it and ask BigAL to compare an impression-only paragraph with an evidence-and-counterevidence paragraph rather than to \"make it more reflective.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I cannot yet credit the gain to the tool because a review day confounds it and the lowest quartile did not move; next unit I will hold difficulty constant and track that subgroup before deciding to keep it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the reflection from a single unit to an end-of-year review of a whole technology initiative. The four moves still govern, but the evidence base widens: a year of data invites trend analysis and the rivals multiply (curriculum changes, new staff, a different cohort). The temptation to credit the initiative for any improvement grows with the stakes. If you understood reflection as disciplined inquiry, you scale the disconfirmation and rival-explanation moves to the larger claim. If you only learned to attach a number to a feeling, the year-end reflection will become a confident endorsement that the messy multi-year evidence cannot actually support.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-06-evidence-based-reflection-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_reflection\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) (Mishra & Koehler, 2006). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2022). The future of AI and education: Some cautionary notes. European Journal of Education. https://doi.org/10.1111/ejed.12532\n- Hanna, A., Denton, E., Smart, A., & Smith-Loud, J. (2020). Towards a critical race methodology in algorithmic fairness. Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency. https://doi.org/10.1145/3351095.3372826\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Williamson, B., Macgilchrist, F., & Potter, J. (2023). Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education. Learning, Media and Technology. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"A reflection on a unit taught with an AI tool reads: \"The lesson went really well and students were engaged.\" Which revision makes it an evidence-based reflection rather than an impression?","options":["\"The lesson went really well and students seemed very engaged and happy.\"","\"Exit-ticket scores rose from 58% to 81%, but the 4 students using the printed fallback averaged 12 points lower, so the tool may have widened a gap I will check next week.\"","\"I felt confident and the energy in the room was high.\"","\"Most students said they liked the tool when I asked.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Evidence-based reflection cites specific observed data, including disconfirming evidence, not feelings or general engagement claims.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_feelings_as_evidence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"A reviewer annotates a reflection: \"You only describe the evidence that supports keeping the tool. What did you see that argued against it?\" What practice does this note ask for?","options":["Add more praise for the tool to balance the tone.","Seek and report disconfirming evidence, so the reflection weighs both supporting and counter-evidence before concluding.","Remove the analysis and keep only the conclusion.","Cite a published study that agrees with the conclusion."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sound reflection actively looks for evidence against its own conclusion, not just evidence that confirms it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_confirmation_only","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher asks BigAL to write a reflection on her own AI-tool lesson. Why does this most undermine the reflection even if the prose is excellent?","options":["The model writes too formally.","A reflection's purpose is to surface the teacher's own observed evidence and reasoning; a model cannot have observed her classroom, so it can only invent plausible claims.","The reflection would be longer than required.","AI reflections are always shorter than human ones."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Reflection depends on the practitioner's own situated evidence; an AI-authored reflection has no observed data to ground it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_ai_invents_evidence","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","stem":"A reflection concludes the AI tool worked because the class average rose 10 points the same week the tool launched. Which reasoning move would make the conclusion more defensible?","options":["Round the gain up to a more impressive number.","Consider rival explanations (a review day, an easier quiz, test practice) before attributing the gain to the tool.","Drop the data and rely on how the lesson felt.","Conclude the tool worked because the gain is large."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Evidence-based reflection checks rival explanations before attributing an outcome to one cause; correlation in one week is weak evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_correlation_as_cause","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_feelings_as_evidence","pattern":"impressions_treated_as_evidence","description":"Reporting feelings, energy, or general engagement as if they were evidence of learning","trigger":"reflection says the lesson went well or students were engaged with no observed data","feedback":"Cite specific observed evidence such as scores, work samples, or subgroup results, not impressions.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_confirmation_only","pattern":"confirmation_only_reflection","description":"Reporting only evidence that supports the preferred conclusion and ignoring counter-evidence","trigger":"reflection lists supporting data but no disconfirming observation","feedback":"Actively report what you saw that argued against your conclusion before deciding.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_ai_invents_evidence","pattern":"reflection_authored_by_ai","description":"Letting AI write a reflection it cannot ground in observed classroom evidence","trigger":"reflection on the teacher's own lesson is generated wholesale by a model","feedback":"Write from your own observed evidence; use AI only to question your reasoning, not to supply the observations.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_6_evidence_based_reflection_correlation_as_cause","pattern":"correlation_attributed_as_cause","description":"Attributing an outcome to the tool because they co-occurred, without checking rival explanations","trigger":"reflection credits the tool for a gain that has obvious alternative causes","feedback":"List and rule out rival explanations before attributing the outcome to the tool.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"93c16f5c5b2ed0c2d03f9d2ceb2d70c0fd11d501ec7cd1e2da32a0249ea90edc","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}